  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Three voltmeters, all having different internal resistances are joined as shown in figure. When some potential difference is applied across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>, their readings are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>. Choose the correct option.</p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/c5aa5b4d-e4a0-4f42-8941-51ccc2212d77.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 146px;\"/>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "8jl0P",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Jhxg5",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "xzXCa",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qOtH4",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let's consider the following diagram:</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/d709c654-7119-4fa3-bb17-1954a8a47e5b.png\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 183px;\"/></p><p>According to the Kirchhoff's voltage law, the algebraic sum of the voltages in a closed loop is zero.</p><p>From the above figure, it can be written that</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
